\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The NSDistributedLock class defines an object that multiple applications on multiple hosts can use to restrict access to some shared resource, such as a file.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The lock is implemented by an entry (such as a file or directory) in the file system. For multiple applications to use an NSDistributedLock to coordinate their activities, the lock must be writable on a file system accessible to all hosts on which the applications might be running.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Use the \b tryLock\b0  method to attempt to acquire a lock. You should generally use the \b unlock\b0  method to release the lock rather than \b breakLock\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSDistributedLock doesn\'27t conform to the NSLocking protocol nor does it have a \b lock\b0  method. The protocol\'27s \b lock\b0  method is intended to block the execution of the thread until successful. For an NSDistributedLock object, this could mean polling the file system at some predetermined rate. A better solution is to provide the \b tryLock\b0  method and let you determine the polling frequency that makes sense for your application.\
\fs14\
