\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(void)\b breakLock\
\fs14\
\b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Forces the lock to be relinquished. This method always succeeds unless the lock has been damaged. If another process has already unlocked or broken the lock, this method has no effect. You should generally use \b unlock\b0  rather than \b breakLock\b0  to relinquish a lock.\
\fs14\
\pard \s67 \li2721 \fi-1209 \ri1007 \ql \tx2721 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Warning:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 Since\b  breakLock\b0  can release another process\'27s lock, it should be used with great caution.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 Even if you break a lock, there\'27s no guarantee that you will then be able to acquire the lock: Another process might get it before your \b tryLock\b0  is invoked.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Raises NSGenericException if the lock could not be removed.\
